12.3  Line endings, BOMs and invisible characters

TL;DR
  - Lines end with \n (LF) on Linux and macOS, \r\n (CRLF) on Windows. The extra
    \r shows as ^M and breaks scripts.
  - A BOM is three invisible bytes (EF BB BF) some Windows tools put at the
    start of UTF-8 files.
  - Copy-pasted text can carry invisible characters: non-breaking spaces,
    zero-width spaces. Commands look right and fail.

      cat -A script.sh | head        # ^M before $ means CRLF; M-BM- is a NBSP
      hexdump -C file | head -2      # the raw bytes
      dos2unix script.sh             # or: sed -i 's/\r$//' script.sh

Pitfalls
  - /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: the script was saved with Windows line
    endings. Convert it, and set * text=auto eol=lf in .gitattributes.
  - A BOM before #! stops the shebang from working, and some JSON parsers reject
    it.
  - A file without a final newline: wc -l counts one line less, and while read
    loops skip the last line.
  - grep -nP '[^\x00-\x7F]' file finds non-ASCII characters hiding in code or
    config.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why two characters for a new line?

    Teletypes needed two moves: carriage return (\r) to bring the print head
    back to the left, line feed (\n) to roll the paper. Windows kept both from
    that era; Unix kept just the line feed; old Macs used \r alone.

  ▾ Why would a UTF-8 file have a byte order mark?

    UTF-16 needs one to tell big-endian from little-endian. UTF-8 has no byte
    order, but Windows tools long added the mark to say "this is UTF-8, not the
    local code page". Most Unix tools treat it as ordinary characters, hence the
    surprises.

Related
  Commands  cat, hexdump, dos2unix, sed
